Z||000024||theogre||02-07-2001||07:30 PM||||Any number of really stupid things can have a big impact on fuel milage. Best thing to do is work your way over and thru the whole motor and inspect everything. <P>Some items to get you started.<P>check the air filter. if it's crappy replace it. I just replaced the air filter on my 4 cyl. I did it in the middle of this tank and already the MPG is up. I'll know just how much after another couple tanks. Dirty air filters are known to chop 2-3mpg off. sometimes more.<P>Look for even small vac leaks. check everything twice.<P>Check the timing even tho it seems to be running right. A small error can be enough to screw up fuel milage.<P>Never use higher octain fuel than your car really needs all your doing is wasting money. Contrary to what the oil companies claim, It can screw up MPG and performance. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Haze_Performance||02-07-2001||07:43 PM||||'85 2.5L Auto. Im getting 190ish out of a full tank. 89oct. All Hwy. driving.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>WS6 Fieros "Members Photo Page"</A>||||reg||13
Z||000026||filthyscarecrow||02-07-2001||10:32 PM||||i get about 21-24 mpg highway. but i have a leak in the fuel return line off the fuel rail from when i swapped out the injectors. i thouhgt the injectors were leaking because the car keps jerking at constant throttle around 25oo rpm. didn't fix the problem. oh well, i didn't like those driveshafts anyway...||||reg||1
Z||000027||Standard||02-08-2001||01:55 AM||||I get about 21mpg combined, maybe a bit higher.  haven't checked it lately.  I know my air filter is dirty.. It did jump up a bit when I went to a 195* stat for this freakin' cold weather.<P>Give the car a tune-up, if it hasn't been done for awhile.  New plugs/wires, cap and rotor, fuel filter, air filter.  Coolant temp sensor and/or the MAT sensor could be bad, too.  Coolant temp sensor for the ECM is under the stat housing, behind the EGR solenoid.  The MAT sensor is in the air filter housing, on the side facing the trunk.  Only sensor in there.||||reg||1
Z||000028||mrfiero||02-08-2001||02:46 AM||||I guess I'll jump in.......My '88 GT auto. (16K miles) gets about 21 MPG.......this is with the TCC disabled.  I'm certain it would get a few MPG's more if it were working (too lazy to replace it).<P>I have owned several auto. '88 GT's and could reach 200 miles on all of them before I had to fill up.  I went a little over 300 miles on one tank.  Overall I would average 22-23 MPG (18-20 city & 25-27 highway).<P>I got 15MPG in an auto. GT while towing another Fiero, so if you can't do better than that without towing something your car has issues that need to be addressed.<P>I have also owned several 5-sp. Formulas.  For the most part my mileage would average 25-27 MPG.  I always got at least 20 MPG and hit 34MPG once on a long trip (went 385 miles on a tank!).  One Formula (yellow with 45K miles) seemed to get worse mileage......I only got 28MPG on a long road trip.  I sold it before I could figure it out.<P>I have also owned an '87 auto. coupe.  It averaged 27-28MPG and I hit 37MPG on a long road trip (went over 400 miles on a tank!!).  I never did worse than 25MPG with that car.<P>I also owned an '85 auto. coupe that didn't fare as well......I got 25-26MPG on the highway and low 20's in town.  <P>I think the newer DIS dukes get better mileage (at least that has been my experience).  <P>The big differences with everyones mileage is due to several factors: climate, tire condition, suspension condition, mechanical shape, how "spirited" you drive, etc.  <P><BR>I can't wait to get my '84 econo coupe running to see what mileage it gets (has 3.32 4-sp.).<P>As they say, "your mileage may vary". ||||reg||10
